2026-10-15 — Area Master shortcodes and human-readable defect references

Implemented required organization-unique Area Master shortcodes, with uppercase A–Z/0–9 normalization and 2–10 character validation. V40 explicitly backfills the known stable codes `CABIN→CBN`, `GALLEY→GLY`, `LAVATORY→LAV`, `ENTRY_AREA→ENT`, and `ATTENDANT_SEAT→ATS`; it aborts if the database contains an unmapped/invalid area or an in-tenant collision. It updates the tenant onboarding area seed function so future organizations receive the same shortcodes. The existing `defects_dtl.reference` field and tenant-unique key from the original schema are retained; its capacity is expanded to 48 characters, and historic EL identifiers are not rewritten.

All new defect creation funnels through `DefectService.create` and `DefectReferenceService`. The reference is `YYYYMMDD-SHORTCODE-NN`; UTC is used as the server business-date policy until the organization has an authoritative timezone setting. PostgreSQL uses a tenant/date/shortcode primary-key counter and atomic `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT ... DO UPDATE ... RETURNING`. The counter allocation, defect row, and initial append-only event share the creation transaction. Standard failed creates roll counter changes back; a concurrent idempotency retry can consume a committed sequence while returning the original defect, so documented sequence gaps can occur but IDs are never reused. Existing idempotency behavior and internal UUID relationships remain intact.

Area Master now displays and edits Shortcode; server filtering, sorting, XLSX export, and the backend CSV template/import flow include the field. The field remains tenant-scoped and existing `ADMINISTER_ORGANIZATION` checks and append-only configuration audit continue to apply. Existing defect screens already render/search/export the API's `reference` property, so they use the generated IDs without duplicating ID-generation logic or changing route UUIDs.

